6. Analytical Questions

Once the full dataset is assembled, the project should test—not assume—the following hypotheses:

H1. Planning capacity: Species with completed and current recovery plans are more likely to demonstrate measurable improvement than species without equivalent planning.

H2. Implementation: Higher levels of documented recovery-action implementation are associated with improved biological trajectories, after accounting for species and threat characteristics.

H3. Time: Recovery outcomes are strongly associated with time since listing and time since major interventions began.

H4. Threat complexity: Species facing multiple interacting threats are less likely to achieve rapid improvement.

H5. Governance complexity: Species whose ranges cross multiple jurisdictions may face greater coordination requirements and potentially greater implementation complexity.

H6. Preventive conservation: Species receiving intervention before severe population decline may require fewer intensive recovery measures later.

These are research hypotheses, not conclusions.

7. Important Analytical Guardrails

The project should avoid several common mistakes.

Do not treat correlation as causation. A species may improve during a period when a recovery action was implemented without that action being the sole or primary cause.

Do not equate funding with effectiveness. More spending may reflect greater biological difficulty rather than poor program performance.

Do not treat delisting as the only meaningful outcome. Stabilization and sustained improvement can be ecologically significant even when legal status remains unchanged.

Do not treat “no change” as failure. A species may improve substantially while remaining below the legal threshold for reclassification.

Do not ignore counterfactuals. Ideally, analysis should ask what would likely have happened without the intervention, although establishing this retrospectively will often be difficult.

Do not mix FWS and NOAA responsibilities without labeling them. FWS and NOAA jointly administer the ESA, with different areas of responsibility. The current quantitative baseline covers species under FWS lead responsibility.

8. Initial Interpretation

The baseline suggests that the central performance problem is unlikely to be captured by a single statistic such as the number of species delisted.

The more useful question is whether the conservation system can reliably:

detect decline early;

identify the causes of decline;

initiate appropriate intervention;

coordinate implementation across jurisdictions and landowners;

measure whether the intervention is working;

adjust when it is not;

establish durable recovery;

verify persistence after formal recovery.

This shifts the wildlife pillar from an ESA compliance model toward a conservation performance model.

Source

U.S. Fish & Wildlife Service. Report to Congress on the Recovery of Threatened and Endangered Species, Fiscal Years 2021–2022. The report covers October 1, 2020 through September 30, 2022 and provides species-level recovery information for 1,589 domestic species under FWS lead responsibility.

FWS also maintains current recovery-plan and species-profile resources through its recovery program and ECOSphere data services.
